iv></div></div></a></figure></div><p>I wrote last week about the emerging p=
attern of intense, =E2=80=9Cagentic=E2=80=9D coding. I work and talk with t=
eams all over the valley that are now deeply into developing with coding fr=
ameworks like Claude Code, or Amplifier. There are many different ways for =
them to work, but there is one pattern that=E2=80=99s very striking, and co=
mmon: the teams are small. Typically, no more than 3 people will work close=
ly together. </p><p><span>At first, I thought this was because they didn=E2=
=80=99t need to: the coding agents are becoming so productive</span><span s=
tyle=3D"min-width: 0px;" data-state=3D"closed"><a data-component-name=3D"Fo=
otnoteAnchorToDOM" id=3D"footnote-anchor-1" href=3D"https://sundaylettersfr=
omsam.substack.com/p/attention-and-collaboration-in-the#footnote-1" target=
=3D"_self" class=3D"footnote-anchor">1</a></span><span> that the individual=
s could get enough work done on their own. To some degree, that=E2=80=99s t=
rue. </span></p><p>But there=E2=80=99s something more interesting going on =
and I think it will impact how we collaborate in general. If you have a sys=
tem like this that is truly =E2=80=9Cagentic=E2=80=9D, in that you can give=
 it complex tasks to work on for long periods of time, it=E2=80=99s very ea=
sy to =E2=80=9Csaturate=E2=80=9D your own attention with ongoing projects. =
This is typically what happens with coding tools - developers build a bunch=
 of projects all at once, and then slow down once there are too many ongoin=
g things to pay attention to (as I write this, I have 9 open Amplifier agen=
ts working). </p><p>If your attention is saturated, though, it=E2=80=99s ve=
ry hard to work with someone else, particularly if their attention is satur=
ated too. You need some spare bandwidth both to generate and consume an int=
eraction with someone else, and that starts to be more painful as you have =
more agents working for you. It feels exactly like managing a large group o=
f people, except in this case, it=E2=80=99s very easy to add agents to the =
mix, so you wind up with a lot of them.</p><p>All of this means that the ov=
erhead of large groups that we are all used to becomes even worse. It=E2=80=
=99s almost as if a 3-person team is really a 30-person team, because of al=
l of the agents involved. In fact, that=E2=80=99s how it feels. </p><p>This=
 pattern happens because the AI coding tools are genuinely acting =E2=80=9C=
as=E2=80=9D people. Not in the sense of AGI or such, but in the sense of be=
ing complex, unpredictable, interactive agents that you have to communicate=
 with. This pattern seems robust to other kinds of work - as soon as an AI =
system is good enough to be treated as a co-worker or agent, it will be. Hu=
man attention will be the bottleneck. </p><p><span>What does this mean for =
collaboration more broadly? It=E2=80=99s not completely clear. It=E2=80=99s=
 likely we need to develop some new muscles and best practices. We probably=
 will continue to try to build tools to manage the attentional load, but I =
suspect we=E2=80=99ll run into </span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w=
iki/Jevons_paradox">Jevon=E2=80=99s paradox</a><span> here: we are likely t=
o keep ourselves saturated no matter how good the tools get. </span></p><p>=
<span>That means we are going to have to start working out how to best work=
 with each other. Making it more efficient to communicate person-to-person =
will likely help. Modular design, both in organizations, artifacts and proc=
esses will be more important. We may see norms that center the tools (or de=
-center them)</span><span style=3D"min-width: 0px;" data-state=3D"closed"><=
a data-component-name=3D"FootnoteAnchorToDOM" id=3D"footnote-anchor-2" href=
=3D"https://sundaylettersfromsam.substack.com/p/attention-and-collaboration=
-in-the#footnote-2" target=3D"_self" class=3D"footnote-anchor">2</a></span>=
</p><p>At the end of the day, no matter how good the tools get, we still ha=
ve to work together. And we have, as humans, a limited amount of attention =
that is always going to be finite. If we are going to get really effective =
impact from these tools, we are going to have to start managing that as the=
 scarce resource that it is. </p><div data-component-name=3D"SubscribeWidge=
